  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Drier cooler weather remains through Wednesday, with next \nrain chances returning Thursday.\n\nHigh pressure and upper level troughing will continue to remain\nover the eastern Conus through Tuesday, and the early portion of\nWednesday. This will keep temperatures slightly cooler than \naverage through the Tuesday. By Wednesday, the upper level \ntrough looks to finally get ejected to the northeast, which will\nstart to raise 500mb heights and subsequently surface\ntemperatures and humidity across the region. As this happens, \nthe surface high pressure that looks to remain quasi-stationary\nacross the region through Tuesday will slowly push east and \nallow the Bermuda surface high to take over. This will pump more\nmoisture back into the region from the western Atlantic, and \nreturn the summer like humid airmass areawide by Wednesday. \n\nAgain on late Wednesday, an upper level shortwave vorticity \nmaxima looks to eject southeastward through the northern Great \nLakes region. This shortwave looks to have a cold front \nassociated with it at the surface that will bring rain chances \nback into the region by Thursday afternoon and evening. At this\ntime, it appears that the mountains will see better chances of\nshowers and thunderstorms based off of the timing of the cold\nfront; however, this could change in the coming days. It's still \ntoo soon at this time to know whether these storms will bring \nthe chance for severe weather or flash flooding, as this will \ngreatly depend on the timing associated with the passage of the \naforementioned cold front. Once CAM guidance comes into range\nstarting late Tuesday, more information will be known on the\noverall severity of these storms, and their potential impacts \nto the region. \n\n\nCool, slightly below-average temperatures will linger through \nTuesday as high pressure and an upper-level trough hold firm \nover the eastern region. By Wednesday, that trough will finally \nshift northeast, allowing heights to rise while the quasi-\nstationary Bermuda high slides eastward. Warm, humid air from \nthe western Atlantic will surge back in under the influence of \nthe Bermuda high, instantly reviving mid-week summer mugginess. \nLate Wednesday into Thursday, an upper-level impulse will sweep \nsoutheast across the northern Great Lakes, dragging a surface \ncold front toward the area. This front will bring a return of \nrain and thunderstorm chances by Thursday afternoon and evening,\nthough the exact timing of its arrival will ultimately \ndetermine any threat of severe weather or flash flooding.",
